  "scientific_interpretation": [
    "The fixed virtual-site fits remain orientation dependent; optimized carbonyl virtual charges often collapse toward zero and donor-site terms do not remove the held-out energy reversals.",
    "The official Drude thymine topology uses asymmetric carbonyl lone pairs, atom-centered polarizabilities, atom-specific Thole screening, and anisotropic carbonyl response. A static equal-site split is not an adequate proxy.",
    "A lesion-only Drude patch inside additive CHARMM36 DNA is prohibited because the published nucleic-acid development reports imbalance and structural drift for mixed or incompletely reoptimized models."
  ],
  "engine_findings": [
    "psfgen 2.0 generated a standard two-MTHY model with 64 particles, including 20 Drude particles, 8 lone pairs, and 4 anisotropy entries.",
    "The installed NAMD 3.0.2 CUDA binary rejects NBTHOLE; the local NAMD Git-2025-12-04 build loads the same PSF and produces a finite zero-step energy.",
    "The full 2018 nucleic-acid topology triggers an unsupported DELETE ANISOTROPY statement in psfgen, so full-DNA construction requires a validated preprocessing fix or CHARMM-GUI/CHARMM-generated PSF."
  ],
  "missing_targets": [
    "B3LYP/aug-cc-pVDZ unperturbed and perturbed ESP maps on the existing MP2/6-31G(d) optimized N-methyl CPD geometry, partitioned into fit and held-out perturbations.",
    "The molecular polarizability tensor and dipole response, with the registered 0.85 condensed-phase polarizability scaling used by the nucleic-acid Drude protocol.",
    "CPD-specific charges, atomic polarizabilities, Thole factors, carbonyl anisotropy, and asymmetric lone-pair charges fitted without changing the standard Drude DNA environment.",
    "Drude-consistent LJ and bonded validation; additive candidate parameters cannot be promoted by direct transfer."
  ],
  "continuation_triggers": [
    {
      "stage": "P1_QM_response",
      "finish_when": "All perturbed/unperturbed ESP and polarizability jobs pass hash, SCF, atom-map, geometry, and finite-tensor audits.",
